{"id":4432,"date":"2021-12-06T15:25:53","date_gmt":"2021-12-06T23:25:53","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4432"},"modified":"2024-12-02T14:48:14","modified_gmt":"2024-12-02T22:48:14","slug":"1-8-while-loops-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/1-8-while-loops-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"1.8: While Loops"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a4c992b\" name=\"6a2213a4c992b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a4c9a88\" name=\"6a2213a4c9a88\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Lesson Goals<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Explain the difference between FOR and WHILE loops<\/li>\n<li>Identify when to use a WHILE loop<\/li>\n<li>Identify the parts of a WHILE loop condition<\/li>\n<li>Create custom loop conditions based on provided values and variables<\/li>\n<li>Create and use random integers in a program<\/li>\n<li>Use the Python console to test conditions<\/li>\n<li>Identify the different logical operators and how they are used in Python programs<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>What are loops?<\/li>\n<li>While Loops<\/li>\n<li>While Loop Practice<\/li>\n<li>Making Comparisons<\/li>\n<li>Checking our Logic<\/li>\n<li>Drone Sways with While Loops<\/li>\n<li>Well, That Was Random!<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: Tower of Terror<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Boomerang<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a4c9afe\" name=\"6a2213a4c9afe\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are Loops?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In the previous lesson, we learned about loops, and more specifically, FOR loops. In this lesson, we will learn about WHILE loops. Both FOR and WHILE loops have the same basic parts:<\/p>\n<p><strong>Loop Condition:\u00a0<\/strong>What causes the loop to continue or to stop. Typically, in the form of an inequality (for example, i &lt; 5).<\/p>\n<p><strong>Loop Body:\u00a0<\/strong>What the loop will do or execute. The steps that the loop will repeat until it is done.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a4c9b63\" name=\"6a2213a4c9b63\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>While Loops<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]The most important difference between for and while loops is their purpose:<\/p>\n<p><strong>For Loops: <\/strong>Use when we have a set number of times we want the loop to run.<\/p>\n<p><strong>While Loops: <\/strong>Use when we don&#8217;t know how many times the loop will run.<\/p>\n<p>See the video below for how to write a while loop and some examples of when we might use while loops:<\/p>\n<p>https:\/\/youtu.be\/z7yLnqTcS0s[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]As you can see, WHILE loops are often used to make our drones seem more intelligent by having them respond to their environments.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a4c9c46\" name=\"6a2213a4c9c46\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>While Loop Practice<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now that we understand what a while loop is and how it works, let&#8217;s look at one of the more important parts: the condition. The condition typically has 3 parts, as shown in the diagram below:[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3716&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221;][vc_column_text]As we see above, the condition compares two values that will result in a <strong>True\u00a0<\/strong>or\u00a0<strong>False\u00a0<\/strong>(a boolean value). The while loop checks this condition each time it runs. If the condition evaluates to True, the loop will continue. If it evaluates to False, the loop will stop.\u00a0 Let&#8217;s practice writing some conditions that we might set up for a while loop.<\/p>\n<p>For the questions below, write out the loop condition only. The first question is given as an example:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The loop will run as long as the variable\u00a0<em>day\u00a0<\/em>is <em>\u201cWednesday\u201d<br \/>\n<\/em><strong>Answer<\/strong>: while day == &#8220;Wednesday\u201d:<\/li>\n<li>The loop will run as long as the variable\u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">home_score<\/code> is greater than the variable\u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">away_score<\/code><\/li>\n<li>The loop will run as long as the variable <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">stop_date<\/code> is not true<\/li>\n<li>The loop will run as long as the variable <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">battery_power<\/code> is greater than 0%<\/li>\n<li>The loop will run as long as the variable\u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">progress_percent<\/code> is less than 100%<\/li>\n<li>The loop will run as long as the variable <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">error<\/code> is False (there are two ways to write this answer; see if you can figure out both)<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a4c9cf2\" name=\"6a2213a4c9cf2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Making Comparisons<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]When creating conditions, we will often want to make different comparisons based on mathematical values. Python uses many of the logical operators that you will see in math class, and they function in the same way.<\/p>\n<p>There are a number of different logical operators:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Greater Than (&gt;) : <\/strong>is the value on the left bigger than the value on the right?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Less Than (&lt;) : <\/strong>is the value on the left smaller than the value on the right?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Equal To (==) : <\/strong>is the value on the left equal to the value on the right?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Not (!) : <\/strong>is the value on the left not the same as the value on the right?<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>You may have noticed that \u201cequal to\u201d is a double equals sign. This is not a typo! Remember that a single equals sign is used to <em>assign <\/em>values, where a double equals is used to <em>test <\/em>whether two values are the same.<\/p>\n<p>We can even combine these operators to make more complex comparisons:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Greater Than or Equal To (&gt;=) : <\/strong>is the value on the left bigger or equal to the value on the right?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Less Than or Equal To (&lt;=) : <\/strong>is the value on the left smaller or equal to the value on the right?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Not Equal To (!=) : <\/strong>is the value on the left not the same as the value on the right?<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a4c9da4\" name=\"6a2213a4c9da4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Checking Our Logic<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Let&#8217;s practice some examples of how these logical tests work! Test each of these examples with a <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">print()<\/code> function. For example, the first one should be:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">print(1&gt;2)<\/pre>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">1&gt;2<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">3&gt;2<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">5&gt;9<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">8&lt;10<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">10 &lt;= 10<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">5 &gt;= 6<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">3==3<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">2==3<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">\u201cHello\u201d == &#8220;Hello\u201d<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">\u201chello\u201d == &#8220;Hello\u201d<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">5 != 4<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">3 != 3<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">!False<\/li>\n<li class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">!True<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Now that we have a better understanding of how these types of comparisons work, let&#8217;s try some exercises on your own. <em>For each question, do you get True, False, or an error?<\/em><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>382 &gt; 438<\/li>\n<li>32&lt;56<\/li>\n<li>True &lt; True<\/li>\n<li>False == False<\/li>\n<li>True !=False<\/li>\n<li>\u201cWorld\u201d = &#8220;world\u201d<\/li>\n<li>&#8220;Codrone&#8221; == &#8220;Codrone&#8221;<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a4c9e0e\" name=\"6a2213a4c9e0e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Swaying with While Loops<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Program your CoDrone EDU to do pushups using a while loop. Have the condition be true only until a variable passes 10.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nsways = 0\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nwhile sways &lt; 3:\r\n\r\n    drone.set_roll(40)\r\n\r\n    drone.move(1)\r\n\r\n    drone.set_roll(-40)\r\n\r\n    drone.move(1)\r\n\r\n    sways += 1   # increment sways by 1\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><em><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">P.S: While loops can go on forever if you set the condition in the while loop to be \u201c1\u201d or \u201cTrue\u201d.<\/span><\/em>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a4c9e97\" name=\"6a2213a4c9e97\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Well, That Was Random!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]An example of running a while loop until it finishes without knowing the end point can be seen using random numbers. To generate random numbers, we use the \u201crandom\u201d library. If you remember libraries from our first lesson, they are just collections of tools that add-on to the Python programming language. In this case, the random library has multiple different functions that generate all sorts of random numbers. The function that we will use is<strong> <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">randint(a,b)<\/code>. <\/strong>In this function, \u201ca\u201d is the starting integer and \u201cb\u201d is the ending integer (very similar to the range function we learned in the for loop lesson). When you call this function, it will generate a random integer that falls between and\/or including \u201ca\u201d and \u201cb\u201d.<\/p>\n<p>Create a file in your lesson directory called &#8220;<em>thats_random.py&#8221;.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Type the following code and run the file:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from random import randint\r\nmyRand = 0\r\nwhile myRand &lt; 10:\r\n    print(myRand)\r\n    myRand = randint(1,10)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Respond to the following questions:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>When you run the program the first time, how many times did the loop execute?<\/li>\n<li>When you run the program again, how many times did the loop execute?<\/li>\n<li>Do the numbers change when you run the program multiple times?<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>As we see with this example, the same loop ran a different number of times each time we ran the program. However, they always stopped when we reached the value 10. This is why while loops are so valuable, no matter how many times the loop executes, all we need to know is what should make the loop stop.<\/p>\n<p>If you want to learn more about the random library in Python, check out the docs <a href=\"https:\/\/docs.python.org\/3\/library\/random.html\">HERE<\/a>.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a4c9f39\" name=\"6a2213a4c9f39\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 1: Tower of Terror<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: Tower of Terror<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The Tower of Terror at a few of the Disney parks is a drop tower ride. These rides typically take riders up very high and very slowly, and then drop very quickly. With a lot of drop tower rides, the riders go down, pause, go up a bit but not that high, pause, go down, pause\u2026you get it.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your challenge is to use loops to program your CoDrone EDU to fly in the same pattern as the Tower of Terror!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><b>Rules:<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You must use either a for or while loop.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your drone, after going up, must go down, pause, go up, and then pause at least three times.<\/span><\/li>\n<li>You must use the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">randomint(a,b)<\/code> function at least 2 times to vary the speed and timing of the drops.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Below is an example<em> (Note: it does not include the random component that you will need to add to your code):<\/em><\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\r\n\r\ndrone = Drone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\u00a0\r\n\r\ndrone.set_throttle(50)\r\n\r\ndrone.move(2)\r\n\r\ndrone.hover(1)\r\n\r\n\r\nfor x in range(0, 5, 1):\r\n\r\n    drone.set_throttle(50)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.move(2)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.hover(1)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.set_throttle(-40)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.move(2)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.hover(1)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a4c9fa1\" name=\"6a2213a4c9fa1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 11<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 2: Boomerang<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Challenge: Boomerang<\/p>\n<p>For this challenge, you will create a program where your drone will fly in 3 random directions and then return to its starting position. For this challenge, you need to meet the following requirements:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Your drone must move in 3 random directions<\/li>\n<li>Your drone must store the values of its movements<\/li>\n<li>Your drone must use the stored values to return to its original starting point<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>You may use the go function or individual movement functions (pitch, yaw, roll, throttle) to make the drone move.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213a4c9ff1\" name=\"6a2213a4c9ff1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 12<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned about while loops and how they differ from for loops. You also learned more about conditions used in loops and how to create conditions based on variables and values. Make sure you know which type of loop to use in each situation![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 13 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/1-8-while-loops-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4734,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,4328,14401,2123,2118,2122,2134],"class_list":["post-4432","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-loops","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4432","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":30,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4432\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13317,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4432\/revisions\/13317"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4734"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4432"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4432"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4432"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}